  "body": {
    "id": "thm:score-lifting-fails-without-chart-richness",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "The answer to \\(\\mathrm{oeq:score-lifting-completeness}\\) is negative under the frozen assumptions.  There is a dominated base law and a bounded likelihood-ratio chart satisfying every displayed operator, rank, closed-range, and bridge-existence condition for which \\(\\mathcal C_P=\\{0\\}\\) but \\(\\mathcal T_P=\\{0\\}\\subsetneq L_0^2(P)=\\mathcal C_P^\\perp\\).  Hence chart richness, not merely operator continuity and locally constant rank, is necessary for reverse score lifting.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "TP",
      "CP"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:dominated-law",
      "ass:outcome-second-moment",
      "ass:local-uniform-equivalence",
      "ass:transported-operator-continuity",
      "ass:fixed-nullity",
      "ass:fixed-cokernel",
      "ass:uniform-reduced-modulus",
      "ass:outcome-bridge-existence",
      "ass:treatment-bridge-existence",
      "def:local-operator-chart",
      "def:joint-model",
      "def:normal-space"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(Z,W,X\\) be constant, let \\(A\\) and \\(Y\\) be independent \\(\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(1/2)\\) variables, and let \\(\\nu\\) be counting measure on the four \\((A,Y)\\)-cells.  Then \\(p=1/4\\) on every cell, so \\(P\\ll\\nu\\) and \\(E[Y^2]=1/2<\\infty\\).  Take the allowed chart domain to be the singleton \\(\\mathcal V_P=\\{P\\}\\), with, for example, likelihood-ratio constants \\(c=1/2\\) and \\(C=2\\).\n\nBoth \\(\\mathcal H\\) and each \\(\\mathcal G_a\\) are one-dimensional.  In scalar coordinates,\n\\[\n T_ah=h,\\qquad T_a^*q=P(A=a)q=q/2.\n\\]\nThus both maps are bijective, have closed range and positive reduced minimum modulus, their transported versions are constant on \\(\\mathcal V_P\\), and their nullities and cokernel dimensions are zero.  The bridges \\(h_a=E[Y\\mid A=a]=1/2\\) and \\(q_a=2\\) exist.  Hence all frozen chart and bridge-existence conditions hold, and \\(P\\in\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{joint}}(P)\\).\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal N_a(P)=\\mathcal K_a(P)=\\{0\\}\\), every generator in \\(\\mathrm{def:normal-space}\\) is zero, whence \\(\\mathcal C_P=\\{0\\}\\) and \\(\\mathcal C_P^\\perp=L_0^2(P)\\).  On the other hand, any path with values in the singleton \\(\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{joint}}(P)=\\{P\\}\\) is constant, so its DQM score is zero and therefore \\(\\mathcal T_P=\\{0\\}\\).  The function \\(s=2Y-1\\) has \\(E[s]=0\\) and \\(E[s^2]=1\\); hence \\(s\\in\\mathcal C_P^\\perp\\) but it cannot be approximated by finite linear combinations of actual path scores.  This is the required explicit witness and disproves the universal reverse inclusion.",
